  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/6/64/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_22.png",
  "plot_summary": "The story opens with Reed running tests on Sue's invisibility power.&nbsp; Predictably, Johnny and Ben get into a tussle that results in the Thing trying to douse Johnny with a canister of chemical foam.&nbsp; Sue is in the direct line of fire, and suddenly, an invisible barrier blocks the foam!&nbsp; Reed is thrilled that she has \"created a shield of invisible energy!\"&nbsp; For the first time, Sue realizes that her invisibility power is a form of energy that she can fashion into a protective screen!\n\nAs Reed encourages her, she fashions a more powerful invisible screen that both Ben and Johnny attempt to penetrate, to no avail.&nbsp; Reed thinks this is only the beginning!&nbsp; Later in the issue, Sue discovers she can make other things invisible too -- though not at the same time she herself is invisible -- she can direct her field of invisibility at only one thing at a time.\n\nA NYPD police officer suddenly enters to report a complaint about the ICBM they keep on the premises, which he thinks violates zoning laws.&nbsp; Suddenly the nearby phones ring, and when Ben answers it, he's beset with complaints about this and that -- noise, sudden shaking of the building, scaring cats, weapons on hand, etc.&nbsp; The FF are making the building undesirable for the other tenants!&nbsp; And a sculptor shows up as well to complain about his sculpture getting melted when the Human Torch flew past his window!&nbsp; The Women's Canasta society shows up to ask them to stop their \"dangerous experiments.\"&nbsp; Reed even fields a call from a \"Mrs. Frobush\" where he says \"I don't know how you got our number, but <i>honest</i>...this <i>isn't</i> Sam's Delicatessen!!\"\n\nAfter Sue helps get rid of the intruders, Reed realizes that Sue never need worry again about feeling as if she didn't contribute enough to the team!&nbsp; He even goes so far to suggest that she might actually be the \"star member!\"&nbsp; But with all the complaints about what they do constantly in the building, Reed also realizes that they do indeed need to find a more secluded spot for some of their larger experiments.&nbsp; He sees a pamphlet about a \"small deserted island for sale off the coast of New Jersey,\" and they take off to go have a look at it.\n\nAs they approach, they see <i>why</i> it's deserted -- surrounded by \"an impenetrable barrier reef.\"&nbsp; It's an eerie place to be sure, and seemingly on cue, their \"u-car\" is pulled underwater, and a figure appears in the shadows.&nbsp; He rushes into a cave as the four pursue.&nbsp; Lo and behold, it's their first enemy, the Mole Man!&nbsp; He welcomes them to his \"kingdom of Subterranea!\"&nbsp; He lured them here with all those complaints from the building tenants, and now they have fallen into his trap, a \"cage\" where the walls are radioactive!\n\nWhen Ben heads over to the wall to climb it, Reed pulls him back, calling him both a \"muscle-bound numbskull\" and a \"lumbering deadhead!\"&nbsp; Reed is obviously upset at himself for leading them into this.&nbsp; Johnny even thinks he's never seen Reed so grim.\n\nThe Mole Man then explains how he escaped underground when Monster Isle was destroyed (back in [LINK:1961:FF_1|FF #1|2026-03-22]).&nbsp; And now he's planning to cause major cities to sink below the Earth, making the US and Soviet Union think the other was responsible, leading to a surface war!&nbsp; But as he reaches for the control device, he discovers that he cannot touch it!&nbsp; Sue has projected an invisible force bubble around it!&nbsp; Reed suggests she surround them instead so that they can approach the wall, shielded by Sue's invisible energy.\n\nNow outside of the cage, they attack!&nbsp; But each is drawn into a room designed to hold them, and the next few pages show each one using their own ingenuity to escape.&nbsp; But even as they emerge to face the Mole Man again, he plunges the room into darkness and uses his \"radar sense\" to evade them.&nbsp; Reed and Ben fumble about in the darkness for a bit before the Torch sheds some light on things.\n\nReed orders them back to the rediscovered \"u-car,\" clearly stressed, telling them they need to go <i>now!</i>  The Mole Man makes his way back to his control device, intending to sink the cities as advertised, but then learns (literally on the very last panel) that Reed has \"rearranged the main circuit wiring so that nothing sinks beneath the Earth's surface except the Mole Man's isle <i>itself</i>, destroying all of his complex scientific equipment in one mad inundating tide of water...\" as the FF escape.",
  "grade_one_liner": "A charming first act and a meaningful upgrade for Sue give the book real lift.\nThe Mole Man's overcooked scheme and abrupt ending keep it from soaring.",
  "subtitle": "A Meaningful Move Forward Mired in Mole Man Mayhem",
  "review_text": "A <i>very</i> difficult one to grade.\n\n[ASSET: left | InvisibleForceField | width=11%] [SPAN: severity=success; text=On the positive side], we have the Invisible Girl <i>finally</i> being given powers enough to actually make a significant contribution to the group.&nbsp; Up until this point, Lee and Kirby seemed to go out of their way to find something she could do.&nbsp; That will no longer be necessary, as she has been given a nice set of abilities.\n\nThey did a great job introducing, then showing off how she can use them.&nbsp; The gradual reveal -- first the force field, then the more delicate energy manipulations -- gives the impression that Sue herself is discovering the limits in real time.\n[ASSET: center | SuesNewPowers | width=67%]\n\nBut there were [SPAN: severity=success; text=<i>more </i>positives] to be found here, namely:\n[HANG] * the playful way that Ben and Johnny were bickering (I especially loved panel 4 on page 2)\n[HANG] * the page 4 cameo appearance by NYPD patrolman Francis Muldoon.&nbsp; Though not named, no one familiar with television at the time could mistake actor Fred Gwynne's face from the TV show <i>Car 54, Where Are You?</i> (which had been canceled by the time this issue came out, but that fact was perhaps as yet unknown to Lee and Kirby at the time of creation).&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: Fred Gwynne would later become known for playing Herman Munster on the 1964-1966 show, <i>The Munsters</i>.]\n[HANG] * the complaints about the ICBM the FF keeps on the premises, which \"violates the <i>zoning </i>law for this neighborhood.\"\n[HANG] * the other absurd complaints that building tenants have about the FF occupying the upper floors of the Baxter Building, which Ben attributes to \"professional <i>gripers</i>.\"&nbsp; It's one of those moments where Ben's grumbling actually feels like the voice of the building itself -- as though the other tenants have formed a union dedicated solely to complaining.\n[HANG] * the Women's Canasta society showing up to ask them to stop their \"dangerous experiments,\" fearful that if anything goes wrong, \"you're liable to make this whole neighborhood radioactive!\" (and right in the middle of their <i>tournament</i> - [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Horrors!])\n\n[ASSET: right | SamsDelicatessen | width=16%] But the highlight of the absurdity comes on page 7, when Reed answers the phone and has to tell \"Mrs. Frobush\" that she has the wrong number and that <i>honestly</i>, [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"This <i>isn't</i> Sam's Delicatessen!!]\"&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: To my knowledge, this is the first time \"Frobush\" is mentioned in a comic, later morphing to the more familiar \"Forbush\" (namely Irving).]\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; text=Reed's absolutely exasperated face is simply <i>classic</i> and made me laugh out loud.]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=It's the kind of interruption that feels like it wandered in from a completely different comic.]\n[BR]\n[BR] The issue later indicates that the various complaints were orchestrated by the Mole Man himself, but you <i>do</i> still have to wonder why things like this hadn't actually come up before.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text= I suspect he didn't really have to twist anyone's arms too much.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nOf course, the many complaints serve only as setup to drive Reed to find a better place to \"conduct our larger experiments,\" which results in him looking at a pamphlet they got \"in the mail <i>today</i>!\" [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(How convenient!)]\n\nIt advertises one of the most unlikely places of all time:&nbsp; \"a small, deserted island for sale, off the coast of New Jersey!\"\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And right before our eyes, the Jersey coastline has somehow become the frontier of uncharted real estate.)]\n\n[ASSET: left | BuyingAnIsland | width=20%] <b><i>Cheap</i></b>, no less, and they <i>immediately</i> race out to buy it!\n[BR]\n[BR] And... please note that, once <i>again</i>, Jack Kirby forgets that the Baxter Building has <i>five</i> upper floors, not <i>four</i> as shown in this panel.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And you gotta <i>love</i> the greenery up on top!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=How come we never <i>see</i> this on roof shots?)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: severity=error; text=On the negative side,] the Mole Man?&nbsp; <b><i>Really</i></b>?\n[ASSET: center | MoleManIsBack | width=67%]\n\nYes, the cover <i>did </i>warn us (the issue advertised it as the FF vs their very first foe), but the Mole Man has never been a particularly memorable one, and his plan to sink the cities and start World War III?&nbsp; Not only does that seem like a [SPAN: severity=info; text=<i>heck</i> of a lot of work], but he also arranged for the Baxter Building \"gripers\" to irritate the Fantastic Four to such an extent that they would seek out a \"deserted island\" to buy off the coast of New Jersey (and even prepared a <b>pamphlet</b> to advertise it, no less).\n\nIt's hard enough to imagine there even <i>being</i> such an island right off the coast of the continental United States!\n\n[ASSET: right | UndergroundHydraulicPlatforms | width=26%] As part of the Mole Man's monologue about how he survived the destruction of Monster Island back in [LINK:1961:FF_1|FF #1|2026-03-22], he states that, in the meantime, he's used his Subterranean subjects to help construct \"huge hydraulic platforms under the largest cities on Earth\" so that when lowered, one Cold War nation will presume that the other attacked it and retaliate with nukes, thus kicking off World War III.\n[BR]\n[BR] You have to admire the Mole Man's <i>astonishing</i> grasp of surface‑world tensions, [SPAN: severity=info; text=where even a technological marvel can be twisted into a doomsday trigger.]\n\nTemporarily putting aside the question of how such huge platforms were constructed [SPAN: severity=warning; text=under the very noses] of a city's populace, did I happen to mention:\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; text=This is a <b>ton</b> of work!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nFrom a storytelling perspective, the narrative lagged between pages 8 through 11, which featured nothing more than the team heading out to his mysterious island, getting onto it, having their craft sunk, and finally encountering the Mole Man.&nbsp; It seemed like a lot of traditional padding that didn't advance the plot much at all.\n\n[ASSET: left | DireSituation | width=36%] The direness of their situation seemed to strike Reed the hardest, and perhaps this explains his crude dialogue and rough language toward the rest of the team, Ben in <i>particular</i>.\n[BR]\n[BR] As for the radioactive cage, we can only assume it was some sort of dome, for otherwise, Johnny could <i>certainly</i> have flown directly out of it, or Reed stretched over it.\n\nBut page 15 seems to suggest that they're able to <i>scale its walls</i>, leaving readers scratching their heads, and forced to somehow mentally reconcile this.&nbsp; It's one of those visual shortcuts Kirby occasionally takes, where the spectacle overrides the mechanics.\n\n[ASSET: right | CantTouchControlDevice | width=10%] It was nice to see how Sue could use her mental control over invisible energy to prevent the Mole Man from activating his control device and later allow the team to safely get over the radioactive walls ([SPAN: severity=success; text=so that's a positive]).\n[BR]\n[BR] But the death traps rolled out over the next several pages were just that:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=death traps], which are visually <i>clever</i>, but nevertheless <i>formulaic</i>, particularly when it comes to the FF.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=You can almost count on it being either a page or a half-page of each one figuring out how to elude them.]\n\nIt's practically a house style at this point and (I'm <i>guessing</i>) only helped eat up six of the seven remaining pages, leaving only one final page to wrap things up.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAnd then... [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Holy sudden stop, Batman!\"]&nbsp; That final page was about as abrupt an ending as I've ever seen before, and that last panel contained so much explanatory text that one can easily imagine artist Jack Kirby sitting at his drawing desk, happily depicting the team members avoiding a whole <i>host</i> of death traps, only to look down at the page number and realize with horror that, [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=\"Dang, this is the last page!\"]\n\nBut did he panic long?&nbsp; <b><i>Heck no!</i></b>\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(He probably figured, \"Well... <i>Stan</i> will figure it out!\")]\n\n[ASSET: center | ScreechingHaltEnding | width=67%]\n\nI can picture Stan Lee getting the finished art and thinking [SPAN: severity=warning; text=\"Thanks for <i>nothing</i>, Kirby!]&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>\n[BR] (The shift from elaborate traps to instant resolution is so sharp it almost feels like a page is missing.)\n\nYes, it was a screeching halt which indicates poor planning on the part of the co-creators, and it does indeed affect the story's final grade.&nbsp; Thanks to the middle panel on page 16, we can at least surmise that Reed had time to rearrange the villain's control device so that it no longer posed any damage to the aforementioned cities, but this all seemed so very \"rabbit out of the hat.\"\n\nBut let's also not forget that the Mole Man's island has now sunk, meaning that Google Maps needs to strike one \"deserted island\" off the coast of New Jersey!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(No <i>wonder</i> I can't find it, <i>heh</i>!)]\n\nAnd I have to ask:&nbsp; who is going to remove those huge, hydraulic platforms from under the cities before the Mole Man can build another control device?&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But I'm probably not supposed to point these things out!&nbsp; <i>Forget I mentioned it!</i>)]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe mixed quality of the issue complicates its grade.&nbsp; The execution was choppy at times, the villain second-rate, and his \"master plan\" to start World War III [SPAN: severity=warning; text=beyond believable], but it still had its moments.\n\n[ASSET: left | Absurdities | width=27%] There's no denying that the first third of the book seems to intentionally showcase a whole <i>host</i> of absurdities.\n[BR]\n[BR] Jack Kirby was obviously having a lot of fun leaning into it, both with the <i>wink, wink</i> cameo by Fred Gwynne, and <i>especially</i> with the different facial expressions, topped by Reed's phone conversation with Mrs. Frobush, not to mention the terror on the faces of the ladies of the Canasta club as they scattered at the top of page 8.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; text=The comedy was top-notch], and it went far beyond the mundane bickering readers <i>normally</i> find dominating the opening pages of this title on a monthly basis.\n\n[ASSET: left | AuntPetunia | width=25%] [ASSET: right | ClobberinTime | width=22%] Ben's first mention of his \"dear ol' [SPAN: severity=info; text=Aunt Petunia]\" (page 12) and subsequent battle cry of [SPAN: severity=info; text= \"It's Clobberin' Time!\"] (page 15) give color and personality to the Thing that continue to make him an enduring member of the team.\n[BR]\n[BR] (Ben has already mentioned having a reputation to maintain, as if he's the \"idol of millions.\")&nbsp; It's an endearing character trait that writer Stan Lee sprinkles into his dialogue.\n\nLet's also not overlook the fact that these pages <i>also </i>do the important work of introducing [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=Sue's new invisibility powers], particularly her ability to cast force fields.\n\nThe fact that this was so seamlessly woven amidst the various neighbors' largely ridiculous complaints about the hazards of living in the same building as the Fantastic Four, not to mention the importance of Sue's contributions to the team, particularly on page 15 -- only [SPAN: severity=success; text=highlights the brilliance of both co-creators], and at the same time almost seamlessly gives the Invisible Girl a <i>major</i> overhaul - [SPAN: severity=info; text=all of which benefits the long-term outlook of the magazine.]\n\nBut of course... [SPAN: severity=error; text= the villain-of-the-month], which the cover so blatantly advertises as \"the first super-villain the FF ever faced!\" [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(like that's somehow a <i>good</i> thing...)], and his dastardly (and <i>way</i> overcomplicated) plan to start World War III was the primary drag on the story.\n\nThat, together with the poor planning on the final page that left poor Stan to pick up the narrative pieces, caps the whole adventure squarely in the mid-grade realm.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=It's a classic case of strong character work carrying a structurally uneven plot.]\n\nAgain, I <i>did</i> appreciate the humor, the personality the dialogue brought to the characters, and the world-building in the form of Sue's new powers.&nbsp; These were all [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=great things] and ensure the book doesn't sink below that mark.\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=But the villain and his master plan...]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=Well, we can only hope the FF are <i>done</i> with the Mole Man for a while!]\n\nDespite the uneven execution, the issue still [SPAN: severity=success; text=marks a meaningful step forward.]&nbsp; Sue's expanded powers aren't just a clever addition - [SPAN: severity=info; text=they reshape the team's dynamic] and hint at a more confident book taking form.&nbsp; That kind of forward motion <i>counts</i> for something, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=even when the villain and plot don't quite rise to meet it.]",
  "letters_page": "This issue features a letter from \"Dave Cockrum\" (at that point apparently a college student in Carbondale, IL), who of course would go on to become a well-known artist and later writer, and a co-creator of the New X-Men.\n\nAnother letter from a guy named \"Roy Thomas,\" who complains that he went to the store and ended up spending a whole $1.95 on comic books -- all of them Marvel!&nbsp; And he points out that he buys two copies of each one!&nbsp; (I hope he held onto them!)\n\nAnd I liked the comment Stephen Erickson made about the <i>Incredible Hulk</i> -- that the first two issues were great \"and ended up a waste of paper.\"&nbsp; (<i>Haha</i>, so true!&nbsp; But I wouldn't have even granted them two issues.)\n\nThe Announcements section explains why they use a limited number of artists (essentially consisting of Jack Kirby, Steve Ditko and Don Heck at this early stage).",
  "ownership": "My own VG/FN or so copy of this book came as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid a little over $20.&nbsp; It ultimately sold on eBay for $139 in Dec 2024, netting me over $98.",
  "highlight": "Sue steps up with a meaningful new ability just as the FF are pulled into the Mole Man's latest overcooked scheme."
}